// Git's smart HTTP protocol, protocol v0 only: enough to fetch a real
// packfile from a real server over `lib/http.src`. See
// `apps/git/design.md`'s "Going remote, 2026-09-29" for where this sits: a
// fetched pack is written to disk here, verified byte for byte against its
// own trailing checksum, and handed to nothing else in this program. Turning
// it into loose objects a repository can actually use is a follow-up, once
// packfile *reading* -- `.idx`, `OBJ_OFS_DELTA`/`OBJ_REF_DELTA` -- exists; it
// is a sibling effort and none of its logic is duplicated here.
//
// API
// ---
//   httpfetch.discover(url)                         -> Result<Advertisement, Error>
//   httpfetch.fetch(url, ad, wants, haves, out_path) -> Result<Summary, Error>
//
//   ad.service, ad.capabilities, ad.refs, ad.object_format
//   summary.pack_bytes, summary.object_count
//
// The seam, testable with no socket in sight (`lib/http.src`'s own reason,
// quoted in its header, holds here too):
//   httpfetch.read_packet(buf, at)      -> Result<Packet, Error>   Part 1
//   httpfetch.pkt_line(payload)         -> bytes                  Part 1
//   httpfetch.parse_advertisement(body) -> Result<Advertisement, Error>  Part 3
//   httpfetch.demux(body)               -> Result<DemuxResult, Error>   Part 4
//   httpfetch.verify_pack(pack, out_path) -> Result<Summary, Error>     Part 5
//
// `discover` and `fetch` are the only two functions that touch a socket, and
// they do it by building a `http.Request` and calling `http.fetch` --
// exactly the same two calls a caller of `lib/http.src` would write by hand.
// Everything else is a pure function over `bytes`, in memory, so the whole
// wire format is unit-testable against fixed byte strings and the two real
// exchanges are tested only for "does a real server's answer parse", not for
// "does the parser work" -- the same split `object.src` draws between
// `read` (I/O) and `parse` (none).
//
// --- protocol version: v0, not v2 -------------------------------------------
//
// A client asks for protocol v2 by sending a `Git-Protocol: version=2`
// header; this module never sends it, so every server answers with v0 (also
// called v1 when a version is stated at all), which every smart-HTTP server
// still speaks as its default or its fallback. v0's pkt-line framing has
// exactly one control packet, the flush (`0000`, no payload); v2 adds a
// delimiter (`0001`) and a response-end (`0002`), and this module refuses
// both as `Syntax` rather than pretending to understand them, since a v0
// server never sends them and misreading one as a length would be silent
// corruption. Protocol v2 is a named follow-up (`apps/git/design.md`), not
// an oversight.
//
// --- the ref advertisement, and what makes it "smart" ------------------------
//
// `GET <url>/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack` gets one of two answers. A
// server that understands the smart protocol sends
// `Content-Type: application/x-git-upload-pack-advertisement`, and the body
// is pkt-lines: `# service=git-upload-pack\n`, a flush, then one ref line per
// pkt-line (the first carrying every capability the server offers, packed
// onto it after a NUL), ending in a flush. A server that does not understand
// the query parameter at all -- an ordinary file server pointed at a bare
// repository, "dumb HTTP" -- answers something else entirely, most often a
// directory listing or a 404, and parsing that as pkt-lines would either trap
// on the length prefix or silently read garbage as ref data. So the content
// type is checked before a single byte of the body is treated as a pkt-line,
// and anything else is `NotSmart`, refused rather than misparsed.
//
// A capabilities line advertising an object format other than SHA-1 --
// `object-format=sha256` -- is `UnsupportedFormat`: `sha1.src`'s own header
// gives the reason there is no second digest in this program, and it holds
// here too.
//
// --- negotiation: the simplest legal round, not the fastest one -------------
//
// This module never requests `multi_ack` or `multi_ack_detailed`, so the
// exchange is git's own "basic" negotiation: every `want` (the first one
// carrying the capabilities this module asks for), a flush, every `have`,
// then `done` -- one round, no back-and-forth reading the server's ACKs in
// between to prune what is sent next. That costs an occasional slightly
// larger `have` list than a smarter client would send; it does not cost
// correctness, and "matches what a modern client negotiates by default" was
// explicitly not the bar (`apps/git/design.md`'s own task note). Computing
// the `have` list itself -- which commits this program already possesses --
// is the caller's job, not this module's: walk `refs.all(gitdir)` and
// `gitlog.walk(gitdir, ref.name, -1)` for each, exactly as `apps/git`'s own
// `-log` does, and hand the object names to `fetch`. For a first `clone`
// that list is empty, which is the simpler case and the one to prove first;
// a `fetch` against a repository that already has history is where `have`
// actually does something, and is the second case this module's own tests
// prove.
//
// **This module requires `side-band-64k`.** Real servers enable it by
// default almost universally (the task's own framing, and every server this
// was tested against), and it is checked against the advertisement rather
// than assumed: a server that does not offer it gets `NoSideBand` rather
// than a demultiplexer fed a stream it was never told to expect. There is
// deliberately no fallback path for a server that omits it -- that path
// would be exercised by nothing this program can reach, and an untested
// branch in a wire parser is worse than no branch.
//
// `ofs-delta` is requested when the server offers it, and `thin-pack` is
// never requested, so the pack this module receives is self-contained:
// every base object a delta needs is inside the pack itself, verifiable with
// nothing else, and indexable by real `git index-pack --stdin` with no
// `--fix-thin`. Whether a delta is `OBJ_OFS_DELTA` or `OBJ_REF_DELTA` is a
// question for the object inside the pack, not for this module, which never
// looks past the pack's own envelope.
//
// --- the ACK/NAK preamble: zero or more lines, not exactly one --------------
//
// The first draft of this module assumed the server answers with a single
// `NAK\n` or `ACK <id>\n` line before the pack stream starts, and it is
// wrong: found by running this against a real `git http-backend` with more
// than one `have` in common. `upload-pack` in stateless mode (smart HTTP
// always uses it, since there is no persistent connection to negotiate over
// several round trips) scans the whole `have` list at once and, without
// `multi_ack`, sends one plain `ACK <id>\n` for **every** common commit it
// finds -- not only the first -- before moving straight into the
// side-band-framed pack. So `skip_ack_nak` reads plain pkt-lines for as long
// as each one's payload starts with `ACK ` or `NAK` (or is a flush, skipped
// as a no-op), and stops -- without consuming it -- at the first packet that
// is neither: that packet is the first side-band pkt-line of the pack
// stream. This can never be ambiguous with real pack data, because a
// side-band payload's first byte is always `1`, `2` or `3`, and neither `A`
// (0x41) nor `N` (0x4e) is one of those.
//
// --- side-band-64k: three channels, one byte each -----------------------------
//
// Once negotiation ends, every remaining pkt-line's payload is `<band><data>`:
// band 1 is pack bytes, appended in order; band 2 is
// progress text, printed to standard error and otherwise ignored -- it is
// not framed into lines and a chunk split across two pkt-lines that happens
// to cut a UTF-8 sequence in half is silently dropped rather than repaired,
// which is the right amount of care for text nothing acts on; band 3 is an
// error from the server's own `upload-pack`, printed to standard error as it
// arrives and turned into `RemoteError` once demultiplexing finishes --
// **never silently swallowed**, which is the one hard requirement on this
// channel. A band outside `1..3`, or a side-band pkt-line with no band byte
// at all, is `BadBand`.
//
// --- verification: the one thing that must never be skipped -----------------
//
// Every packfile ends with a 20-byte SHA-1 of everything before it.
// `verify_pack` computes that digest itself (`sha1.src`) and refuses to
// write anything to disk if it does not match, or if fewer than 32 bytes
// arrived, or if the first four are not `PACK` -- a truncated or corrupted
// transfer is `Truncated`, `BadPack` or `ChecksumMismatch`, never a pack on
// disk that looks fine and is not. The write itself never touches the
// target path directly: a temporary file beside it, written whole, then
// renamed over -- the same discipline `refs.src` and `object.src` already
// use, for the same reason.
//
// --- what is NOT here ---------------------------------------------------------
//
//   - **Unpacking.** `Summary.object_count` is read straight out of the
//     pack's own 12-byte header (`PACK`, a version, a count) as a sanity
//     number to report; nothing here parses a single object out of the pack,
//     resolves a delta, or writes a loose object. That is the sibling
//     packfile-reading task's own `.idx`/`OBJ_OFS_DELTA`/`OBJ_REF_DELTA`
//     work, and this module does not depend on any of it being done.
//   - **Push**, and the capabilities that only matter for it
//     (`report-status`, `delete-refs`, `push-options`, ...).
//   - **Protocol v2** -- see above.
//   - **SSH**, and TLS: `lib/http.src`'s own `parse_url` already refuses
//     `https://` before a socket exists, for the reason its header gives,
//     and that refusal is inherited here rather than repeated.
//   - **`multi_ack`/`multi_ack_detailed`** -- see "negotiation" above.

import http;
import io;
import fs;
import object;
import random;
import sha1;

/// Why a fetch or a ref listing could not be completed. Payload-free
/// (`docs/errors-decision.md` §4, the convention every module in this
/// program past `zlib`/`refs`/`object`/`http` already follows): the URL, the
/// bytes involved, and which capability or band was wrong are context the
/// caller already has when it reports.
pub enum Error {
    /// The HTTP exchange itself failed -- a bad URL, a refused, reset or
    /// timed-out connection, or a malformed HTTP message. `lib/http.src`'s
    /// own `Error` already said which; there is nothing more specific to add
    /// at this layer.
    Http;
    /// The server answered, but not with `200`.
    Status;
    /// The response's `Content-Type` does not name a smart-HTTP git service
    /// -- most likely a plain file server, "dumb HTTP", answering the same
    /// URL a git server would.
    NotSmart;
    /// The ref advertisement is not shaped the way `info/refs` promises: no
    /// `# service=...` line, no flush where one is required, or a ref line
    /// this module cannot parse.
    BadAdvertisement;
    /// A pkt-line's length prefix is not four hex digits, or names `1`, `2`
    /// or `3` -- protocol v2's delimiter and response-end packets, which a
    /// v0-only client never sends and never expects back.
    Syntax;
    /// A pkt-line's length prefix names more bytes than actually arrived --
    /// a message cut off in the middle.
    Truncated;
    /// The server's object format is not SHA-1. See `sha1.src`'s own note on
    /// why there is no second digest here.
    UnsupportedFormat;
    /// The ref advertisement does not offer `side-band-64k`, which this
    /// module requires -- see the module note.
    NoSideBand;
    /// A side-band pkt-line named a channel other than 1, 2 or 3, or carried
    /// no band byte at all.
    BadBand;
    /// Band 3 carried an error from the server's own `upload-pack`, already
    /// printed to standard error by the time this is returned.
    RemoteError;
    /// The bytes that were meant to be a packfile do not begin with the
    /// `PACK` magic.
    BadPack;
    /// The pack's trailing SHA-1 does not match the SHA-1 of what came
    /// before it: a truncated or corrupted transfer.
    ChecksumMismatch;
    /// `fetch` was given no `want`s -- there is nothing to negotiate.
    NoWant;
    /// A `want` or a `have` is not a well-formed object name: 40 lowercase
    /// hex digits.
    BadName;
    /// The verified pack could not be written to disk.
    Io;
}

pub str Error.to_str() {
    match (this) {
        case Http: {
            return "the HTTP exchange failed";
        }
        case Status: {
            return "the server did not answer 200";
        }
        case NotSmart: {
            return "the server answered dumb HTTP, not a smart-HTTP git service";
        }
        case BadAdvertisement: {
            return "the ref advertisement is not well-formed";
        }
        case Syntax: {
            return "the pkt-line framing is not well-formed, or is protocol v2's";
        }
        case Truncated: {
            return "the message was cut off in the middle";
        }
        case UnsupportedFormat: {
            return "the server's object format is not SHA-1";
        }
        case NoSideBand: {
            return "the server did not offer side-band-64k, which this module requires";
        }
        case BadBand: {
            return "a side-band pkt-line named an unknown channel";
        }
        case RemoteError: {
            return "the server reported an error; see standard error";
        }
        case BadPack: {
            return "the bytes received are not a packfile";
        }
        case ChecksumMismatch: {
            return "the pack's trailing checksum does not match its content";
        }
        case NoWant: {
            return "no want was given -- nothing to fetch";
        }
        case BadName: {
            return "not a well-formed object name: 40 lowercase hex digits";
        }
        case Io: {
            return "the pack could not be written to disk";
        }
    }
}

// --- pkt-line framing (Part 1) -----------------------------------------------

/// One packet read out of `buf` at `at`: `flush` with no payload, or `data`
/// with its payload -- the bytes strictly after the four-hex-digit length
/// prefix, which counts itself. `next` is the offset just past this packet,
/// for the caller's next call; there are no tuples in this language
/// (`apps/git/FRICTION.md` §2), so a small result type carries both answers
/// out of one call the way `gitlog.Entry` does.
pub type Packet {
    pub bool flush;
    pub bytes payload;
    pub int next;
}

/// `"0000"`, as bytes -- the one control packet this (v0-only) module knows.
pub const bytes FLUSH_PKT = [48, 48, 48, 48];

/// Read one pkt-line at `buf[at ..]`.
///
/// A length of `0000` is a flush, with no payload. Any other length must be
/// at least 4 (it counts its own four digits) and no more than what remains
/// in `buf`; `1`, `2` and `3` are protocol v2's delimiter and response-end
/// packets and are refused as `Syntax` rather than misread as a one-, two- or
/// three-byte payload, since this module never negotiates v2 and a real v0
/// server never sends them.
pub Result<Packet, Error> read_packet(bytes buf, int at) {
    if (at < 0 || at + 4 > buf.size()) {
        return Result<Packet, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    int len = 0;
    for (int i in 0 .. 4) {
        int d = hex_val(buf[at + i]);
        if (d < 0) {
            return Result<Packet, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
        len = len * 16 + d;
    }
    if (len == 0) {
        return Result<Packet, Error>.Ok(Packet(true, [], at + 4));
    }
    if (len < 4) {
        return Result<Packet, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    int end = at + len;
    if (end > buf.size()) {
        return Result<Packet, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    bytes payload = buf.substr(at + 4, end);
    return Result<Packet, Error>.Ok(Packet(false, payload, end));
}

/// Encode `payload` as one pkt-line: its length (itself included), in four
/// lowercase hex digits, then the payload verbatim. Never used for a flush --
/// callers append `FLUSH_PKT` directly.
pub bytes pkt_line(bytes payload) {
    bytes out = len_hex(payload.size() + 4);
    out.extend(payload);
    return out;
}

// The length-prefix trick: two big-endian octets, then `bytes.hex()` --
// which zero-pads every octet to two digits -- gives exactly the four hex
// digits a pkt-line length is, with no hand-written digit table.
bytes len_hex(int n) {
    bytes two = [n >> 8 & 0xFF, n & 0xFF];
    return two.hex().to_bytes();
}

int hex_val(int c) {
    if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {
        return c - 48;
    }
    if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') {
        return c - 87;
    }
    if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F') {
        return c - 55;
    }
    return -1;
}

const bytes SP1 = [32];
const bytes NUL1 = [0];
const bytes LF1 = [10];

// --- the ref advertisement (Part 3) ------------------------------------------

/// One advertised ref: its full name and the object it points at. A tag's
/// peeled entry (`refs/tags/v1^{}`) is advertised as an ordinary ref of this
/// shape with that literal name -- git's own convention, not a second
/// mechanism -- and is not treated specially here.
pub type RemoteRef {
    pub str name;
    pub str id;
}

/// The service announcement, the capabilities on the first ref line, and
/// every ref -- in the server's own order, which is what `git ls-remote`
/// prints and what this module's own tests compare against byte for byte.
pub type Advertisement {
    pub str service;
    pub List<str> capabilities;
    pub List<RemoteRef> refs;
    pub str object_format;
}

/// `GET <url>/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack`, parsed.
///
/// Refuses `NotSmart` before looking at the body at all if the response is
/// not `200` with `Content-Type: application/x-git-upload-pack-advertisement`
/// -- see the module note on why a dumb-HTTP answer must never reach the
/// pkt-line parser.
pub Result<Advertisement, Error> discover(str url) {
    str target = base_of(url) + "/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack";
    match (http.request("GET", target)) {
        case Ok(http.Request req): {
            req.set("accept", "application/x-git-upload-pack-advertisement");
            match (http.fetch(req)) {
                case Ok(http.Response resp): {
                    return after_refs_response(resp);
                }
                case Err(http.Error e): {
                    return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Err(Error.Http);
                }
            }
        }
        case Err(http.Error e): {
            return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Err(Error.Http);
        }
    }
}

Result<Advertisement, Error> after_refs_response(http.Response resp) {
    if (resp.status() != 200) {
        return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Err(Error.Status);
    }
    str ctype = resp.header("content-type").or("");
    if (!ctype.starts_with("application/x-git-upload-pack-advertisement")) {
        return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Err(Error.NotSmart);
    }
    return parse_advertisement(resp.body());
}

/// The ref advertisement, parsed out of the response body already read into
/// memory -- no I/O, no socket, exactly `object.parse`'s split from
/// `object.read`. Exposed on its own so the framing rules can be checked
/// against a captured or hand-built advertisement with no server involved.
pub Result<Advertisement, Error> parse_advertisement(bytes body) {
    Packet first = read_packet(body, 0)?;
    if (first.flush) {
        return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
    }
    str line0 = "";
    match (first.payload.utf8()) {
        case Some(str s): {
            line0 = s.trim();
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
        }
    }
    if (!line0.starts_with("# service=")) {
        return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
    }
    str service = line0.substr(10, line0.size());
    Packet flush1 = read_packet(body, first.next)?;
    if (!flush1.flush) {
        return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
    }
    int at = flush1.next;
    List<str> caps = [];
    List<RemoteRef> refs = [];
    bool first_ref = true;
    while (true) {
        Packet p = read_packet(body, at)?;
        at = p.next;
        if (p.flush) {
            break;
        }
        bytes line = p.payload;
        if (line.ends_with(LF1)) {
            line = line.substr(0, line.size() - 1);
        }
        if (first_ref) {
            first_ref = false;
            match (line.index_of(NUL1)) {
                case Some(int nul): {
                    bytes capbytes = line.substr(nul + 1, line.size());
                    line = line.substr(0, nul);
                    match (capbytes.utf8()) {
                        case Some(str s): {
                            for (str c in s.split(" ")) {
                                if (c.size() > 0) {
                                    caps.push(c);
                                }
                            }
                        }
                        case None: {
                            return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
                        }
                    }
                }
                case None: {
                }
            }
        }
        RemoteRef r = parse_ref_line(line)?;
        if (r.name != "capabilities^{}") {
            refs.push(r);
        }
    }
    str object_format = "sha1";
    for (str c in caps) {
        if (c.starts_with("object-format=")) {
            object_format = c.substr(14, c.size());
        }
    }
    if (object_format != "sha1") {
        return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedFormat);
    }
    return Result<Advertisement, Error>.Ok(Advertisement(service, caps, refs, object_format));
}

Result<RemoteRef, Error> parse_ref_line(bytes line) {
    int sp = 0;
    match (line.index_of(SP1)) {
        case Some(int i): {
            sp = i;
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<RemoteRef, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
        }
    }
    if (sp != 40) {
        return Result<RemoteRef, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
    }
    str id = "";
    match (line.substr(0, 40).utf8()) {
        case Some(str s): {
            id = s;
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<RemoteRef, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
        }
    }
    if (!object.is_name(id) || id.size() != 40) {
        return Result<RemoteRef, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
    }
    str name = "";
    match (line.substr(41, line.size()).utf8()) {
        case Some(str s): {
            name = s;
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<RemoteRef, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
        }
    }
    if (name.size() == 0) {
        return Result<RemoteRef, Error>.Err(Error.BadAdvertisement);
    }
    return Result<RemoteRef, Error>.Ok(RemoteRef(name, id));
}

bool has_cap(List<str> caps, str name) {
    for (str c in caps) {
        if (c == name || c.starts_with(name + "=")) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// --- negotiation and the fetch itself (Part 4) -------------------------------

/// What was demultiplexed out of a side-band-64k response: the reassembled
/// pack bytes (band 1), and whether band 3 carried an error -- already
/// printed to standard error by the time this is returned, per the module
/// note; `remote_error` is what tells the caller to stop rather than go on
/// to verify a pack that was never meant to be trusted.
pub type DemuxResult {
    pub bytes pack;
    pub bool remote_error;
}

/// Demultiplex a side-band-64k byte stream: every pkt-line is
/// `<band><data>`, band 1 accumulates into `pack`, band 2 is printed to
/// standard error and discarded, band 3 is printed to standard error and
/// sets `remote_error`. `body` starts right after the ACK/NAK preamble --
/// `fetch` skips that (zero or more lines; see the module note) with
/// `skip_ack_nak`, using the same `read_packet` this takes its input from, so
/// this function is pure demultiplexing and nothing about negotiation.
pub Result<DemuxResult, Error> demux(bytes body) {
    int at = 0;
    bytes pack = [];
    bool saw_error = false;
    while (at < body.size()) {
        Packet p = read_packet(body, at)?;
        at = p.next;
        if (p.flush) {
            continue;
        }
        if (p.payload.size() == 0) {
            return Result<DemuxResult, Error>.Err(Error.BadBand);
        }
        int band = p.payload[0];
        bytes data = p.payload.substr(1, p.payload.size());
        if (band == 1) {
            pack.extend(data);
        } else if (band == 2) {
            match (data.utf8()) {
                case Some(str s): {
                    io.eprint(s);
                }
                case None: {
                }
            }
        } else if (band == 3) {
            saw_error = true;
            match (data.utf8()) {
                case Some(str s): {
                    io.eprint("remote: " + s);
                }
                case None: {
                    io.eprint("remote: (an error, not valid UTF-8)");
                }
            }
        } else {
            return Result<DemuxResult, Error>.Err(Error.BadBand);
        }
    }
    return Result<DemuxResult, Error>.Ok(DemuxResult(pack, saw_error));
}

/// What a fetch produced: the verified pack's size on disk, and the object
/// count read out of its own 12-byte header (`PACK`, a version, a count) --
/// a number this module reports and does not itself check against the
/// pack's actual contents, since counting them means parsing them, which is
/// the sibling packfile-reading task's job, not this one's.
pub type Summary {
    pub int pack_bytes;
    pub int object_count;
}

/// `POST <url>/git-upload-pack`: negotiate for `wants` given `haves`
/// (computed by the caller -- see the module note), and write the verified
/// pack to `out_path`.
///
/// `ad` is the `Advertisement` `discover` already returned for this `url`:
/// its capabilities decide what this module asks for (`ofs-delta` only if
/// offered; `side-band-64k` always, and `NoSideBand` if the server never
/// offered it). Every `want` and `have` must be a 40-digit lowercase hex
/// object name, and there must be at least one `want`.
pub Result<Summary, Error> fetch(str url, Advertisement ad, List<str> wants, List<str> haves, str out_path) {
    if (wants.size() == 0) {
        return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.NoWant);
    }
    for (str w in wants) {
        if (!object.is_name(w) || w.size() != 40) {
            return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.BadName);
        }
    }
    for (str h in haves) {
        if (!object.is_name(h) || h.size() != 40) {
            return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.BadName);
        }
    }
    if (!has_cap(ad.capabilities, "side-band-64k")) {
        return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.NoSideBand);
    }
    bytes body = negotiation_body(ad, wants, haves);
    str target = base_of(url) + "/git-upload-pack";
    match (http.request("POST", target)) {
        case Ok(http.Request req): {
            req.set("content-type", "application/x-git-upload-pack-request");
            req.set("accept", "application/x-git-upload-pack-result");
            req.set_body(body);
            match (http.fetch(req)) {
                case Ok(http.Response resp): {
                    return after_pack_response(resp, out_path);
                }
                case Err(http.Error e): {
                    return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.Http);
                }
            }
        }
        case Err(http.Error e): {
            return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.Http);
        }
    }
}

// The request body: every `want` (capabilities on the first only, per the
// protocol's own rule), a flush, every `have`, then `done` -- git's basic,
// single-round negotiation; see the module note on why there is no
// `multi_ack`.
bytes negotiation_body(Advertisement ad, List<str> wants, List<str> haves) {
    List<str> use = ["side-band-64k"];
    if (has_cap(ad.capabilities, "ofs-delta")) {
        use.push("ofs-delta");
    }
    use.push("agent=lang-httpfetch/1");
    str first_caps = use.join(" ");
    bytes out = [];
    bool first = true;
    for (str w in wants) {
        if (first) {
            out.extend(pkt_line(("want " + w + " " + first_caps + "\n").to_bytes()));
            first = false;
        } else {
            out.extend(pkt_line(("want " + w + "\n").to_bytes()));
        }
    }
    out.extend(FLUSH_PKT);
    for (str h in haves) {
        out.extend(pkt_line(("have " + h + "\n").to_bytes()));
    }
    out.extend(pkt_line("done\n".to_bytes()));
    return out;
}

Result<Summary, Error> after_pack_response(http.Response resp, str out_path) {
    if (resp.status() != 200) {
        return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.Status);
    }
    str ctype = resp.header("content-type").or("");
    if (!ctype.starts_with("application/x-git-upload-pack-result")) {
        return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.NotSmart);
    }
    bytes body = resp.body();
    // Zero or more plain ACK/NAK lines, then the side-band pack stream --
    // see the module note for why "zero or more" and not "exactly one".
    int at = skip_ack_nak(body)?;
    match (demux(body.substr(at, body.size()))) {
        case Ok(DemuxResult d): {
            if (d.remote_error) {
                return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.RemoteError);
            }
            return verify_pack(d.pack, out_path);
        }
        case Err(Error e): {
            return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(e);
        }
    }
}

// The offset just past the ACK/NAK preamble: every plain `ACK ...`/`NAK...`
// line (and any flush, a harmless no-op wherever it appears in this
// implementation), for as long as the next packet still looks like one.
// The first packet that does not is left unconsumed -- it is the first
// side-band pkt-line of the pack stream, and `demux` reads it next.
Result<int, Error> skip_ack_nak(bytes body) {
    int at = 0;
    while (at < body.size()) {
        Packet p = read_packet(body, at)?;
        if (p.flush) {
            at = p.next;
            continue;
        }
        if (!is_ack_or_nak(p.payload)) {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(at);
        }
        at = p.next;
    }
    return Result<int, Error>.Ok(at);
}

bool is_ack_or_nak(bytes payload) {
    match (payload.utf8()) {
        case Some(str s): {
            return s.starts_with("ACK ") || s.starts_with("NAK");
        }
        case None: {
            return false;
        }
    }
}

// --- verification (Part 5) ---------------------------------------------------

/// Check `pack`'s trailing 20-byte SHA-1 against the SHA-1 of everything
/// before it (`sha1.src`), and only then write it to `out_path`. Exposed on
/// its own -- given bytes, not a URL -- so a truncated or corrupted transfer
/// can be tested by handing this function a byte string that was never near
/// a socket: a real pack with its tail cut off, or with one byte of its
/// checksum flipped, must come back `Truncated`/`ChecksumMismatch` and must
/// never reach `write_atomic`.
pub Result<Summary, Error> verify_pack(bytes pack, str out_path) {
    if (pack.size() < 32) {
        return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.Truncated);
    }
    if (pack[0] != 'P' || pack[1] != 'A' || pack[2] != 'C' || pack[3] != 'K') {
        return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.BadPack);
    }
    int count = be32(pack, 8);
    bytes content = pack.substr(0, pack.size() - 20);
    bytes trailer = pack.substr(pack.size() - 20, pack.size());
    if (sha1.sum(content) != trailer) {
        return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.ChecksumMismatch);
    }
    match (write_atomic(out_path, pack)) {
        case Ok: {
            return Result<Summary, Error>.Ok(Summary(pack.size(), count));
        }
        case Err(io.Error e): {
            return Result<Summary, Error>.Err(Error.Io);
        }
    }
}

int be32(bytes b, int at) {
    return b[at] << 24 | b[at + 1] << 16 | b[at + 2] << 8 | b[at + 3];
}

// --- small shared helpers -----------------------------------------------------

str base_of(str url) {
    if (url.ends_with("/")) {
        return url.substr(0, url.size() - 1);
    }
    return url;
}

// Write `content` to `path` without ever leaving a half-written file there --
// a freshly named temporary file beside it, written whole, then renamed over
// the target. The same discipline `refs.src`'s own `write_atomic` uses, for
// the same reason; duplicated here rather than imported, since `refs.src`'s
// copy is private to that module (`apps/git/FRICTION.md`'s `before`/`str_cmp`
// are the same trade already made twice in this program).
Result<bool, io.Error> write_atomic(str path, bytes content) {
    match (fs.makedirs(dir_of(path))) {
        case Ok: {
        }
        case Err(io.Error e): {
            return Result<bool, io.Error>.Err(e);
        }
    }
    random.System rnd = random.system();
    str tmp = path + ".tmp" + rnd.hex(12);
    match (io.write_bytes(tmp, content)) {
        case Ok: {
        }
        case Err(io.Error e): {
            return Result<bool, io.Error>.Err(e);
        }
    }
    return fs.rename(tmp, path);
}

str dir_of(str path) {
    int i = path.size() - 1;
    while (i >= 0 && path.byte_at(i) != '/') {
        i = i - 1;
    }
    if (i < 0) {
        return ".";
    }
    return path.substr(0, i);
}
